{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "am-05-adding-pure-ingredient-water",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Adding Pure Ingredient or Water",
    "description": "Freeze the component that does not change, then solve the new ratio after adding only one pure ingredient.",
    "teachingApproach": "Begin with a two-part mixture where adding pure water changes only water. Use the untouched component as an anchor, then solve a one-line ratio equation. Progress through adding milk, adding water, percentage targets, reverse initial-quantity problems, and capstones that translate between ratio and percentage.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 28,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 21, Alligation or Mixture. Approved scope: Teach that adding a pure component changes only that component's quantity while the other component stays fixed. Cover adding milk to raise the milk : water ratio, adding water to lower concentration, converting a target percentage to a target ratio, finding how much pure component is needed, and working backwards from a changed ratio to the original quantity. Include patterns like a 7 : 3 milk-water mixture receiving water until water becomes 40%, and a 4 : 3 mixture receiving a known amount of water and becoming 8 : 7. Learners should freeze the untouched component first, then solve the one small ratio equation that remains."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's change a mixture by adding only one pure component.\n\nIf we pour water into a milk-water mixture, the milk amount does not change. That unchanged amount becomes our anchor, and most questions collapse to one small ratio equation.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A mixture has milk:water = 3:1, total 20 litres.\n\nMilk = 15 L, water = 5 L.\n\nIf we add only water, the milk stays 15 L. That fixed 15 L is our anchor.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Suppose we want milk:water = 3:2.\n\nWith milk fixed at 15 L, water must be 10 L.\nIt is currently 5 L, so add 5 L water.\n\nThe untouched component turns the problem into a small target-ratio calculation.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
