        "text": "A common slip here is to mix up which number is a price, quantity, or fraction.\n\nInitial salt=12 L. Solve (12+x)/(30+x)=1/2.\n\n24+2x=30+x, so x=6 L.\n\nNow use the same idea on fresh values.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "retry_4"
        }
      ]
    },
    "retry_remedy_4": {
      "id": "retry_remedy_4",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's rebuild this one from the quantities in the question.\n\n6+x=9.6+0.4x, so 0.6x=3.6 and x=6 L.\n\nTry it again with that relationship in view.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "retry_4"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_5": {
      "id": "teach_5",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Some questions run backwards.\n\nMilk:water = 4:3. Add 6 L water and the new ratio becomes 8:7.\n\nLet original milk=4x and water=3x. After adding water: 4x:(3x+6)=8:7.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_5_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_5_step": {
      "id": "teach_5_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "7×4x = 8(3x+6)\n28x = 24x+48\n4x=48 → x=12.\n\nOriginal milk = 4x = 48 L.\n\nThe added component creates the only change in the ratio equation.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_5"
        }
      ]
    },
